{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794586374,"nonce":"oLvndYC57XNtigW9yQJwT9N0P00=","title":"A baked frame with more primitives than the buffer aborts the process instead of truncating","message":"Found while chasing 0e62d2e. With every row of a 20,000-row flat list inside the viewport and BLINC_MOTION_PROMOTION=1, the first frame aborts on a wgpu validation error: a write of 23.7 MB into a 4.8 MB buffer. 4.8 MB is 20,000 primitives at 240 bytes, the default capacity, so the batch had about 99,000 primitives. The default path in the same situation logs \"Primitive count N exceeds buffer capacity 20000, truncating\" and carries on (write_primitives_safe, and the partial write clips the same way); the baked path has a write that does not clip. The write site is not identified yet.\n\nReproducer: build scroll_bench as it was before ef27c2a4 (git show ef27c2a4^:examples/blinc_app_examples/examples/scroll_bench.rs), then LIST=flat ROWS=20000 BLINC_MOTION_PROMOTION=1. The example no longer reaches it, because its list now culls. Any frame that paints more than max_primitives in baked mode should, e.g. a scroll container with viewport_cull(false) over a long list.\n\nExpected: the same truncation and warning as the default path, not an abort.","files":null}]}